00:34:39,974 --> 00:34:44,680
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, as the Buddha said, the conditions for faith, well...

339
00:34:45,810 --> 00:34:49,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Reflect on... Do you actually feel a sense of urgency?

340
00:34:49,853 --> 00:34:57,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you actually feel, as the Buddha said, that... you know, for somebody who hasn't realized the Four Noble Truths, it's like they...

341
00:34:58,199 --> 00:35:03,043
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, because what should somebody do if their head is on fire?

342
00:35:03,163 --> 00:35:12,550
[SPEAKER_02]: He said they should apply unwavering effort, diligence, recollection, perseverance, in order to extinguish that fire.

343
00:35:13,250 --> 00:35:25,314
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, well, somebody might actually let the fire be, not pay attention to it, and it would actually be more important to make an effort to discern the four noble truths.

344
00:35:26,255 --> 00:35:40,599
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's obviously kind of a hyperbole in the sense, but it goes against a common kind of understanding that, oh, we just have to be satisfied with where you are right now.

345
00:35:40,819 --> 00:35:41,280
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know,

346
00:35:42,820 --> 00:35:55,608
[SPEAKER_02]: All these attainments and stuff, these are just more forms of becoming, and so it's more important to just accept yourself as you are now, and then just be with that, and that's how you... Just deal with it, yeah.

347
00:35:55,988 --> 00:36:02,272
[SPEAKER_02]: But the Buddha is saying you should see your situation as if your head is on fire.

348
00:36:03,112 --> 00:36:04,173
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so that's clearly a problem.

349
00:36:04,293 --> 00:36:04,453
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

350
00:36:09,055 --> 00:36:37,303
[SPEAKER_03]: I wanted to maybe, like, briefly go back to, like, this kind of tendency of trying to find justification, like, with the teaching or with anything else, but in this context with the teaching, and I wanted to ask, like, someone can, like, listen to this discussion and say, okay, from now on, I will stop... make justification for myself, but that's kind of changing the things to, like, kind of an opinion level, but not yet to the view, so I wanted to ask, like,

351
00:36:38,698 --> 00:36:49,300
[SPEAKER_03]: I think it will be kind of relevant to kind of make this distinction between like what an opinion, a belief is, and what the point of view is, because it's quite a different thing.

352
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:52,680
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean, eh, justify, like, in terms of actions?

353
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:53,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

354
00:36:58,161 --> 00:37:07,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, it's when... the thing is, always then, if you have that attitude of

355
00:37:09,332 --> 00:37:10,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's just... it's just this.

356
00:37:10,933 --> 00:37:12,953
[SPEAKER_02]: I just correct this and I'm already in the right path.

357
00:37:13,993 --> 00:37:21,396
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whichever shape that takes, if you don't assume, like, okay, I'm not justifying myself anymore, but do you stop there?

358
00:37:21,976 --> 00:37:26,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you keep looking, like, okay, but... well, do I know the extent of action?

359
00:37:27,838 --> 00:37:31,959
[SPEAKER_02]: For example, like, sure, I'm restraining my obvious

360
00:37:32,980 --> 00:37:39,822
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, yeah, you accept, like, you have, like, an opinion of, yeah, I shouldn't act out of this.

361
00:37:50,626 --> 00:37:53,489
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's something that you can acquire in just a moment.

362
00:37:53,509 --> 00:37:56,931
[SPEAKER_02]: You just read a book, it says, okay, don't do this, and that's it.

363
00:37:57,151 --> 00:38:16,727
[SPEAKER_02]: But changing your view takes much longer, takes training, takes upgrading your criteria for what even... like the terms that you're using to begin with, like wholesome and wholesome, craving, abandoning, mind, consciousness,

364
00:38:18,141 --> 00:38:22,229
[SPEAKER_02]: Are those... like, do you feel like that's it?

365
00:38:22,871 --> 00:38:26,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do you recognize, actually, even those things, I cannot be sure.

366
00:38:27,319 --> 00:38:27,800
[SPEAKER_02]: I understand.

367
00:38:27,840 --> 00:38:29,003
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, when I'm saying...

368
00:38:29,849 --> 00:38:32,691
[SPEAKER_02]: I am purifying my mind from unwholesome states.

369
00:38:33,351 --> 00:38:38,434
[SPEAKER_02]: That might not mean for me what it should mean, for example.

370
00:38:39,575 --> 00:38:54,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's how you see that there is that extra dimension between you just accepting an opinion from something you read or something somebody told you, and then developing or adjusting your view according to it.

371
00:38:55,025 --> 00:38:56,526
[SPEAKER_02]: There's that extra dimension of like

372
00:38:57,607 --> 00:39:04,883
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, you agree with what they said, but how are you actually interpreting it internally?

373
00:39:04,983 --> 00:39:07,349
[SPEAKER_02]: So, five aggregates, you know.

374
00:39:09,353 --> 00:39:10,915
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a common example.

375
00:39:11,115 --> 00:39:28,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, people talk about the five aggregates, but they never stop to question the fact that maybe their definition of what five aggregates constitutes is either inaccurate or too vague or just overall not really referring to what the Buddha pointed to with these things.

376
00:39:29,792 --> 00:39:39,780
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what faith would allow you to consider, at least, that there's that constant improvement that needs to be made, constant questioning.

377
00:39:40,381 --> 00:39:46,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Even for a noble disciple, it's not a matter of just, okay, now I just mechanically do this practice.

378
00:39:47,687 --> 00:39:56,494
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha said even, you cannot become an anāgāmī, so a sotāpanna would not be able to become an anāgāmī if they lack faith.

379
00:39:58,133 --> 00:40:05,679
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... obviously, they have unshakable faith, but it can always be increased and deepened further.

380
00:40:06,260 --> 00:40:06,580
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

381
00:40:07,982 --> 00:40:10,924
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like I said, it's not something you increase directly.

382
00:40:10,964 --> 00:40:12,085
[SPEAKER_02]: It's through clarification.

383
00:40:12,145 --> 00:40:16,668
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first, that sense of urgency.

384
00:40:16,708 --> 00:40:27,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I covering up the sense of urgency as well, through distractions and sensuality, which makes me feel like everything is all right, finding safety in the world?

385
00:40:27,575 --> 00:40:28,176
[SPEAKER_02]: That's one way.

386
00:40:28,416 --> 00:40:30,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, also, developing clarity.

387
00:40:30,897 --> 00:40:33,439
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's when, then, it's not just about faith.

388
00:40:33,679 --> 00:40:34,560
[SPEAKER_02]: You also have to

389
00:40:36,661 --> 00:40:48,107
[SPEAKER_02]: Clarify, for example, read the suttas, and clarify the teaching, so that it doesn't feel anymore like this vague, ambiguous, kind of, yes, I have to give a craving, but I don't really know what that means.

390
00:40:49,227 --> 00:40:54,070
[SPEAKER_02]: You make it more, sort of, concrete, and then you get a sense of what you need to do.

391
00:40:54,690 --> 00:41:00,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And that increases your faith in a sense, because you see, oh wait, it's not even like this mystical.

392
00:41:01,294 --> 00:41:07,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you mystify things, and you make them kind of... put them too far away from you, then that's also not faith anymore.

393
00:41:07,258 --> 00:41:09,699
[SPEAKER_02]: It needs to be faith.

394
00:41:09,839 --> 00:41:13,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, it's over there, but I also have a recognition of what I can do.

395
00:41:14,522 --> 00:41:16,524
[SPEAKER_02]: At least to bring me closer to that direction.

396
00:41:17,185 --> 00:41:21,169
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha called that measuring the task.

397
00:41:21,749 --> 00:41:24,853
[SPEAKER_02]: You measure what it is that you actually need to do.

398
00:41:25,413 --> 00:41:33,301
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, on account of that, the faith, or you could say the desire, or the zeal to do it arises.

399
00:41:34,128 --> 00:41:37,490
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's a good point, because sometimes faith can be an excuse to do something.

400
00:41:37,950 --> 00:41:42,192
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I just have faith, which I'm practically not doing anything.

401
00:41:42,292 --> 00:41:46,715
[SPEAKER_04]: But what we're talking about is, the faith is precisely what ennobles you to go there.

402
00:41:46,875 --> 00:41:47,435
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

403
00:41:47,555 --> 00:41:50,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's not just, oh, this, nibbāna...

404
00:41:50,897 --> 00:42:01,966
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and then the light in that faith, which is sometimes like devotion, but there can be an attitude of inauthenticity, just making excuses like, yeah, I have devotion, but practically you're not really doing anything.

405
00:42:02,006 --> 00:42:06,329
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, devotion can result from faith, but not the other way around, necessarily.

406
00:42:06,570 --> 00:42:06,750
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

407
00:42:06,850 --> 00:42:16,478
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because you would still see, you know, in the suttas, like how the monks would be, even when they were arahants, they would say, like, somebody would come and ask them a question,

408
00:42:17,699 --> 00:42:20,722
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... they would say... well, you shouldn't ask me.

409
00:42:20,742 --> 00:42:22,124
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not the one who knows.

410
00:42:22,204 --> 00:42:22,824
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the teacher.

411
00:42:23,245 --> 00:42:24,586
[SPEAKER_02]: He's... the Buddha is the one who knows.

412
00:42:25,467 --> 00:42:27,149
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... even though they were arahants.

413
00:42:27,630 --> 00:42:30,272
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you could see that, yes, there was a sense of devotion there.

414
00:42:30,853 --> 00:42:32,114
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you just fabricate that...

415
00:42:33,395 --> 00:42:34,336
[SPEAKER_02]: It really makes sense.

416
00:42:34,356 --> 00:42:35,417
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just hollow, yeah.

417
00:42:36,257 --> 00:42:42,161
[SPEAKER_04]: But perhaps it can also be helpful to clarify a bit, like, in order to, like, find a starting point.

418
00:42:42,682 --> 00:42:53,029
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, faith in the suttas, even with the Buddha, it is like seeing a dead person just in the streets, somebody who is old or sick.

419
00:42:53,969 --> 00:42:55,771
[SPEAKER_04]: That is what, for the Buddha,

420
00:42:56,451 --> 00:42:59,354
[SPEAKER_04]: Kindled that search, that faith.

421
00:42:59,994 --> 00:43:07,561
[SPEAKER_04]: So, suffering is not like, oh, I have particular problems, like my child is misbehaving, or my marriage is going down the drain.

422
00:43:07,581 --> 00:43:08,681
[SPEAKER_04]: These are all particular things.

423
00:43:09,062 --> 00:43:10,743
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, that's all unpleasant.

424
00:43:11,084 --> 00:43:14,907
[SPEAKER_04]: But actually, that faith is on the level, as we said earlier, of past existence.

425
00:43:14,947 --> 00:43:18,109
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, even if everything is now going according to your wishes,

426
00:43:18,770 --> 00:43:22,152
[SPEAKER_04]: You're still subject to old age, sickness, death.

427
00:43:22,712 --> 00:43:25,313
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is where the hair is on fire.

428
00:43:25,353 --> 00:43:28,355
[SPEAKER_04]: So that faith, in its nature, covers

429
00:43:29,759 --> 00:43:35,702
[SPEAKER_04]: The whole of your life, it's not this particular situation or this particular problem that is now occupying your mind.

430
00:43:37,202 --> 00:43:39,303
[SPEAKER_04]: It's much bigger picture.

431
00:43:41,024 --> 00:43:47,606
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's why we said it would have to have an effect on the way you live your life, if it's genuine faith.

432
00:43:47,787 --> 00:43:50,648
[SPEAKER_04]: It cannot just remain as an empty conviction.

433
00:43:50,668 --> 00:43:58,111
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so people can ask, is the fact that I'm subjected to all day sickness, death, genuinely a problem for me?

434
00:43:58,875 --> 00:43:59,155
[SPEAKER_04]: Or not.

435
00:44:00,036 --> 00:44:09,980
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if somebody then thinks, well, this is the only life I just try to live, to sit out of it as comfortably as possible, and then that's it, that's going to be an obstruction to faith as well.

436
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:13,641
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'd be surprised how many Buddhists kind of have that view.

437
00:44:13,721 --> 00:44:25,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's, it's, oh, well, you know, it's, it's, eh, I'll just do the best in this life, and then, eh, whether they believe in rebirth or not, they might say, oh, well, I'll just

438
00:44:25,848 --> 00:44:31,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Get reborn in the next one and do my best and... Then I'll do the work.

439
00:44:31,233 --> 00:44:31,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

440
00:44:31,853 --> 00:44:33,995
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like right now I have family, I have children.

441
00:44:34,015 --> 00:44:34,976
[SPEAKER_02]: I cannot do it right now.

442
00:44:35,016 --> 00:44:35,677
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll do it later.

443
00:44:36,517 --> 00:44:44,945
[SPEAKER_02]: And, yeah, that means... Sure, you still recognize the value in an ambiguous sense of enlightenment.

444
00:44:45,846 --> 00:44:53,052
[SPEAKER_02]: But I like to compare that to like somebody who, let's say, they already earn a hundred thousand per year.

445
00:44:53,740 --> 00:44:56,001
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, they have the option to gain one million.

446
00:44:56,861 --> 00:44:58,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, sure, I would like to do that.

447
00:44:59,322 --> 00:45:09,166
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if it takes too much of my effort and too much of my energy, I might just rest comfortable with what I already have.

448
00:45:10,946 --> 00:45:17,869
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, that's kind of the interpretation that a lot of people have behind like stream entry and all these things.

449
00:45:18,689 --> 00:45:20,610
[SPEAKER_02]: But, it's not something that you're gaining.

450
00:45:21,532 --> 00:45:24,835
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a huge problem that you need to lose.

451
00:45:25,736 --> 00:45:26,977
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your head being on fire.

452
00:45:27,777 --> 00:45:36,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you see it in that way, that's not something that you can just kick your legs up and say, okay, I'll just stay with my head on fire.

453
00:45:36,484 --> 00:45:44,631
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if it doesn't feel like that kind of

454
00:45:47,060 --> 00:45:50,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, basically, there isn't anything else, really, that's worth it, apart from this.

455
00:45:51,303 --> 00:45:58,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you haven't recognized sufficiently the perilous situation that saṃsāra is.

456
00:45:58,627 --> 00:45:59,148
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

457
00:45:59,348 --> 00:46:06,012
[SPEAKER_04]: So, with the proper faith, you cannot say, I'm doing this practice, but there's also something of equal importance next to it.

458
00:46:06,032 --> 00:46:06,632
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly, yeah.

459
00:46:06,692 --> 00:46:08,694
[SPEAKER_03]: That cannot be.

460
00:46:08,734 --> 00:46:08,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

